Proof Type Mismatch - ATTEMPTED BUT UNEXPLOITABLE

This test was an attempt to exploit the proof irrelevance bug (HIGH-2) where
isProofIrrelevantEq doesn't check that proof types match.

SETUP:
  P : Prop           -- A proposition
  Q : Prop           -- Another proposition
  hp : P             -- Proof of P
  hq : Q             -- Proof of Q
  T : P -> Type      -- Type family indexed by P proofs
  v : T hp           -- Value of type T hp

  def foo : T hq := v   -- SHOULD FAIL: v : T hp, but foo declared as T hq

EXPECTED BUG BEHAVIOR:
  When checking foo, trepplein should compare T hq with T hp.
  In checkDefEqCore, both are Apps with same head (T).
  It compares hq with hp via checkDefEq(hq, hp).
  isProofIrrelevantEq(hq, hp) = isProof(hq) && isProof(hp) = true && true = true
  The bug: this returns IsDefEq without checking that P = Q!

ACTUAL BEHAVIOR:
  Trepplein CORRECTLY rejects this export, but for a different reason.
  When inferring the type of the declared type expression "T hq",
  it calls checkType(hq, P) because T : P -> Type.
  This fails because infer(hq) = Q and Q ≠ P.

CONCLUSION:
  The proof irrelevance bug exists in the code but is protected by earlier
  type inference checks. When forming an application App(f, arg), trepplein
  verifies that arg has the correct type for f's domain. This catches the
  ill-typedness before the proof comparison happens.

  The bug may be unexploitable in practice, but it should still be fixed
  for correctness - the isProofIrrelevantEq function violates the invariant
  that it should only return true for proofs of the SAME proposition.

VALIDATION:
  nanoda_lib: REJECTS (panic at expr.rs:593)
  trepplein:  REJECTS (wrong type: hq : P, inferred: Q)

  Both correctly reject - this is NOT a differential bug.
